  "ai": {
    "status": "ok",
    "model": "deepseek-chat",
    "summary": "The candidate change sets DEMO_STARTUP_FAIL=true in Deployment demo/demo, which the FAULT_INJECTION rule blocks. This is a deliberate startup-failure drill, not a production release: the trusted cases describe it as validating rolling-update protection (maxUnavailable: 0 / maxSurge: 1), where the new Pod CrashLoops while the old Pod stays Ready and serving. The decision to BLOCK stands and must not be overridden. Reviewers should also note that the old Pod remaining Ready does not prove the new version is healthy, and that capacity figures are uncertain because 35 scheduled Pods lack CPU/memory requests while KubeCPUOvercommit is firing.",
    "additional_risks": [
      {
        "title": "Old Pod readiness can mask a failed new version",
        "detail": "Because the rollout keeps the previous Pod Ready and serving, a green deployment status or healthy probes do not show that the new Pod started successfully. Release verification must check new-Pod logs/events for startup_failed and CrashLoopBackOff rather than relying on the old Pod's 200 responses.",
        "severity": "REVIEW"
      },
      {
        "title": "Capacity headroom may be overstated during surge",
        "detail": "The rollout adds a surge Pod alongside the retained old Pod, but 35 scheduled Pods have no CPU/memory requests and are counted as zero, and KubeCPUOvercommit is firing. Free-capacity numbers should not be treated as reliable for this change.",
        "severity": "REVIEW"
      }
    ],
